# Module Overview 

Thinking Critically About AI is organized around a set of **core ideas about how AI works and how humans should interact with it responsibly**. Rather than walking through every lesson individually, this professional learning module focuses on the most important concepts, instructional moves, and project structures that support student learning across the unit.

## What's Included in This Module? 

This professional learning module is organized into **three sections**:

<div style="background-color: #FAFBFC; padding: 20px; border-radius: 10px; width: 88%; margin: 20px auto;">

  <p style="font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 0;">
    💻 Lesson 1: Introduction to Thinking Critically About AI
  </p>
  <p style="font-size: 16px;">
    This section orients you to the unit, including its structure, student experience, learning tools, computational thinking practices, and instructional goals.
  </p>

  <br>

  <p style="font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;">
    🧠 Lesson 2: Big Ideas in Thinking Critically About AI
  </p>
  <p style="font-size: 16px;">
    This section explores the major concepts students investigate throughout the unit, including how AI generates outputs, why AI can be unreliable, how testing and evidence support responsible use, why context matters, and how students examine risks related to bias, privacy, and misinformation.
  </p>

  <br>

  <p style="font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;">
    📑 Lesson 3: The Generate and Judge Project
  </p>
  <p style="font-size: 16px; margin-bottom: 0;">
    This section focuses on the Generate and Judge Guide, including how the project develops across the unit, how students collect and organize evidence, and how teachers can support revision, reflection, and evidence-based conclusions about responsible AI use.
  </p>

</div>

<div style="border-top: 1px solid #048899; margin: 30px 0px;"></div>

This professional learning module is designed to prepare you to teach **Thinking Critically About AI** through its most important ideas and instructional moments. You can revisit sections throughout your planning and teaching as needed.
MARKDOWN
